<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <!--
      Calendar with a clock, over the brand purple.

      One amber body, the color carried over from the pre-2.0 icon. Everything that would
      otherwise be a second color is a hole instead, so the shapes read the same way in the
      full-color and themed variants.

      Sizing: the group scale lands the artwork on roughly the 60dp keyline circle. Adaptive
      icon artwork should not fill the whole 72dp safe zone (no breathing room under a
      circular mask), nor rely on that zone's square corners (they get clipped).

      For the themed (monochrome) variant everything flattens to a single tint, so the
      calendar keeps an evenOdd clearance hole around the clock; without it the two shapes
      merge into one blob.
    -->
    <group
        android:pivotX="54"
        android:pivotY="54"
        android:scaleX="0.68"
        android:scaleY="0.68">

        <!--
          Calendar and clock as a single evenOdd path.

          Every shape here is one subpath of one path, and evenOdd turns any region covered an
          even number of times into a hole. That is what draws the line under the calendar
          header, the clearance gap between the calendar and the clock, and the clock's hands.

          One path rather than two because holes only work between subpaths of the same path.

          Subpaths, in order:
            1-2  the two tabs on top of the calendar
            3    the calendar body
            4    the header line — a hole
            5    the clock face, center (71,71) radius 13.5
            6    the hands, reading 3 o'clock — a hole

          There is no separate clearance shape between the calendar and the clock. Where the
          two overlap they cover the same region twice, and evenOdd turns that into a hole:
          the crescent gap falls out of the geometry rather than being a third circle sized
          by hand. The clock sits far enough down and right that the crescent follows the
          calendar's corner instead of biting into its side.

          One path, not two, because a hole only works between subpaths of the same path —
          and a hole punched into a subpath that is itself a full circle does not survive the
          resource build at all, whatever the fill rule says.

          The hands are a gap rather than a painted shape so they survive the monochrome
          variant, where the launcher flattens this whole layer to a single tint and a painted
          hand would vanish into the face. An L of two bars stays legible once the glyph is
          shrunk to 48dp; two thin strokes at an arbitrary angle would not.
        -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFA51E"
            android:fillType="evenOdd"
            android:pathData="M37,28 a2.5,2.5 0 0,1 2.5,2.5 v5 h-5 v-5 a2.5,2.5 0 0,1 2.5,-2.5 z
                              M55,28 a2.5,2.5 0 0,1 2.5,2.5 v5 h-5 v-5 a2.5,2.5 0 0,1 2.5,-2.5 z
                              M35,32 h24 a6,6 0 0,1 6,6 v27 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,6 h-24 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,-6 v-27 a6,6 0 0,1 6,-6 z
                              M29,43 h36 v4 h-36 z
                              M57.5,71 a13.5,13.5 0 1,0 27,0 a13.5,13.5 0 1,0 -27,0 z
                              M69.2,60.5 h3.6 v8.7 h8.5 v3.6 h-12.1 z" />
    </group>
</vector>
